      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this OIML certificate carefully.\n\n**Certificate-level info:**\n- Certificate No.: R49/2013-A-CZ1-24.02\n- Scheme: A\n- Member State: Czech Republic\n- Date issued: 6 June 2024\n- OIML Issuing Authority: Czech Metrology Institute, Okružní 31, 638 00 Brno, Czech Republic\n- Person responsible: Jan Kalandra\n- Page total: 7 pages\n- Recommendation: OIML R 49, Edition 2013\n- Accuracy class: 2\n\n**Issuing authority:**\n- Name: Czech Metrology Institute\n- Address: Okružní 31, 638 00 Brno, Czech Republic\n- Person responsible: Jan Klandra\n- There's also \"RNDr. Pavel Klenovský, Head of Certification Body\" who signed — that could be person_responsible for signing. The first page says \"Person responsible: Jan Kalandra\". The signature block says RNDr. Pavel Klenovský, Head of Certification Body. I'll use Jan Kalandra as person_responsible (explicitly labeled) and maybe add person_title... Actually the schema has person_responsible and person_title. Page 1: Person responsible: Jan Kalandra. Page 2 signature: RNDr. Pavel Klenovský, Head of Certification Body. Hmm. I'll go with Jan Kalandra as person_responsible. Could also add signed_by info but schema doesn't have it. I could put person_responsible: \"Jan Kalandra\". The title isn't given for him. Alternatively use Klenovský with title \"Head of Certification Body\". The schema fields: name, address_lines[], person_responsible, person_title, phone, email, website, oiml_issuer_id. I think person_responsible = \"Jan Kalandra\" is the safest since it's explicitly labeled \"Person responsible\".\n\n**Applicants:**\n- GWF AG, Obergrundstrasse 119, CH-6005 Luzern, Switzerland\n\n**Manufacturers:**\n- GWF AG, same address\n\n**Certified type:**\n- Category: Water meter (ultrasonic) — schema examples: \"Load cell\", \"Indicator\", \"Measuring system\", \"Fuel dispenser\", \"Taximeter\", \"Gas meter\"... For water meter, \"Water meter\" seems right.\n- type_designations: [\"sonico NANO\"]\n- module_designation: \"-\" → Not applicable / null\n- description: the measuring system description\n\n**Model family:**\nThe cert covers multiple variants: nominal diameter 15 and 20, various Q3 values (1.6, 2.5, 4.0 m³/h) and Q3/Q1 ratios (40 to 1000). This is a family of models. The flowrates tables define 28 combos for DN15 and 28 for DN20? Let me count.\n\nDN15 table 1: Q3/Q1 ratios 40, 50, 63, 80, 100, 125 — each ratio has two Q3 values (1.6 and 2.5). So 12 variants.\nDN15 table 2: ratios 160, 200, 250, 315, 400, 500 — 12 variants.\nDN15 table 3: ratios 630, 800, 1000 — 4 variants (630 has 2: 1.6 and 2.5; 800 has 1: 2.5; 1000 has 1: 2.5).\nTotal DN15: 12+12+4 = 28 variants.\n\nDN20 table 1: ratios 40,50,63,80,100,125 — 12 variants (Q3 2.5 and 4.0 each).\nDN20 table 2: ratios 160,200,250,315,400,500 — 12 variants.\nDN20 table 3: ratios 630,800,1000 — 4 variants (630: 2.5; 800: 4.0; 1000: 4.0).\nTotal DN20: 28 variants.\n\nTotal: 56 variants.\n\nAlso the pressure loss class varies per model: DN15: Q3 1.6 → Δp10; Q3 2.5 → Δp16 (from page 4 column for DN15). Wait, let me re-read:\n\n```\n<tr><td><em>Pressure loss class:</em></td><td align=\"center\"><em>Q</em>3 1.6 <em>Δp</em>10<br><em>Q</em>3 2.5 <em>Δp</em>16</td><td align=\"center\"><em>Q</em>3 2.5 <em>Δp</em>16<br><em>Q</em>3 4.0 <em>Δp</em>25</td></tr>\n```\n\nThe first column corresponds to DN15: Q3 1.6 → Δp10, Q3 2.5 → Δp16. Second column corresponds to DN20: Q3 2.5 → Δp16, Q3 4.0 → Δp25.\n\nConnection type: DN15: NPSM or G type ¾\", 7/8\", 1\"; DN20: NPSM or G type 7/8\", 1\", 1 1/4\".\n\nLength: ≥105 mm for both.\n\nQ2/Q1 = 1.6, Q4/Q3 = 1.25 — type level.\n\nTemperature class: T50. Water pressure class MAP16. Max admissible temp 50°C. Max admissible pressure 1.6 MPa. Environmental class M. Electromagnetic environment: E2 (marking says E1 — inconsistency in the doc; the characteristics table says E2; marking section says E1. I'll use E2 from characteristics table). Actually the marking section lists \"Electromagnetic environmental class (E1)\" while the characteristics table says E2. The characteristics table is authoritative for the certified characteristics. I'll record E2.\n\nAccuracy class 2. MPE lower zone ±5%, upper zone ±2% (≤30°C), ±3% (>30°C).\n\nIndicating range testing/user mode: 9 999 / 999 999 m³. Resolution indicating device: 0.00001/0.001 m³. Resolution of device for rapid testing: 100 pulse/dm³. Resolution indicating device rapid testing: 0.000001 m³.\n\nEUT testing requirements: Category B, Case B.\n\nMin straight length inlet/outlet: 0 mm. Flow profile sensitivity class U0D0. Flow conditioner: No. Mounting: -. Orientation: any.\n\nPower supply: Battery, Umax 3.6 V, Umin 1.9 V. Battery life: 16 years. Software version 0.6.28. CRC checksums: 0x854EBACF, 0x13372073.\n\n**Flowrate matrix tables:**\n\nTable 1 (DN15, ratios 40-125):\n- Q1: 0.040, 0.063 | 0.032, 0.050 | 0.025, 0.040 | 0.020, 0.031 | 0.016, 0.025 | 0.013, 0.020\n- Q2: 0.064, 0.100 | 0.051, 0.080 | 0.041, 0.064 | 0.032, 0.050 | 0.026, 0.040 | 0.021, 0.032\n- Q3: 1.600, 2.500 (×6)\n- Q4: 2.000, 3.125 (×6)\n- Q3/Q1: 40, 50, 63, 80, 100, 125\n\nTable 2 (DN15, ratios 160-500):\n- Q1: 0.010, 0.016 | 0.008, 0.013 | 0.006, 0.010 | 0.005, 0.008 | 0.004, 0.006 | 0.003, 0.005\n- Q2: 0.016, 0.025 | 0.013, 0.020 | 0.010, 0.016 | 0.008, 0.013 | 0.006, 0.010 | 0.005, 0.008\n- Q3: 1.6, 2.5 ×6\n- Q4: 2.0, 3.125 ×6\n- Ratios: 160, 200, 250, 315, 400, 500\n\nTable 3 (DN15, ratios 630-1000):\n- Q1: 0.0025, 0.004 | 0.003 | 0.0025 — hmm. Let's parse: `<td>0.0025</td><td>0.004</td><td>0.003</td><td>0.0025</td>` with ratio row: `630` (colspan 2), `800`, `1000`. So:\n  - Ratio 630: Q1 = 0.0025 (Q3=1.6), Q1 = 0.004 (Q3=2.5)\n  - Ratio 800: Q1 = 0.003 (Q3=2.5)\n  - Ratio 1000: Q1 = 0.0025 (Q3=2.5)\n  - Q2: 0.004, 0.006 | 0.005 | 0.004\n  - Q3: 1.600, 2.500 | 2.500 | 2.500\n  - Q4: 2.000, 3.125 | 3.125 | 3.125\n\nTable 4 (DN20, ratios 40-125):\n- Q1: 0.063, 0.100 | 0.050, 0.080 | 0.040, 0.064 | 0.031, 0.050 | 0.025, 0.040 | 0.020, 0.032\n- Q2: 0.100, 0.160 | 0.080, 0.128 | 0.064, 0.102 | 0.050, 0.080 | 0.040, 0.064 | 0.032, 0.051\n- Q3: 2.500, 4.00 ×6\n- Q4: 3.125, 5.00 ×6\n- Ratios: 40, 50, 63, 80, 100, 125\n\nTable 5 (DN20, ratios 160-500):\n- Q1: 0.016, 0.025 | 0.013, 0.020 | 0.010, 0.016 | 0.008, 0.013 | 0.006, 0.010 | 0.005, 0.008\n- Q2: 0.025, 0.040 | 0.020, 0.032 | 0.016, 0.026 | 0.013, 0.020 | 0.010, 0.016 | 0.008, 0.013\n- Q3: 2.5, 4.0 ×6\n- Q4: 3.125, 5.00 ×6\n- Ratios: 160, 200, 250, 315, 400, 500\n\nTable 6 (DN20, ratios 630-1000):\n- Q1: 0.004, 0.006 | 0.005 | 0.004\n- Q2: 0.006, 0.010 | 0.008 | 0.006\n- Q3: 2.500, 4.00 | 4.00 | 4.00\n- Q4: 3.125, 5.00 | 5.00 | 5.00\n- Ratios: 630, 800, 1000\n\nSo models are identified by (DN, Q3, Q3/Q1). I'll create model_ids like \"DN15_Q3=1.6_R=40\" or \"sonico NANO DN15 Q3=1.6 Q3/Q1=40\". Let me use a compact id: \"DN15-Q3 1.6-R40\"? Better something readable: \"sonico NANO DN15 Q3=1.6 Q3/Q1=40\". Hmm, that's long for 56 models. Maybe \"DN15/Q3=1.6/R=40\".\n\nActually, the schema suggests model_id like \"G1.6\". For this cert, the natural key is DN + Q3 + ratio. Let me use `model_id: \"DN15-Q3 1.6-R40\"`? Or \"DN15 Q3=1.6 Q3/Q1=40\". I'll go with \"DN15_Q3=1.6_R=40\".\n\nHmm, let me think about what's most useful. The matrix_tables will contain the full data with rows per variant. The model_level characteristics would duplicate it. Given the instruction to populate both for R137-style tables, I should do both. But 56 models × 6 attributes is a lot. The instructions say to do it for the R137 example. Let me generate them.\n\nModel list:\n\nDN15 (Q3 = 1.6 for ratios 40,50,63,80,100,125,160,200,250,315,400,500,630; Q3 = 2.5 for ratios 40..800... wait no.\n\nDN15:\n- R40: Q3 1.6 (Q1 0.040, Q2 0.064, Q4 2.000), Q3 2.5 (Q1 0.063, Q2 0.100, Q4 3.125)\n- R50: Q3 1.6 (0.032, 0.051, 2.000), Q3 2.5 (0.050, 0.080, 3.125)\n- R63: Q3 1.6 (0.025, 0.041, 2.000), Q3 2.5 (0.040, 0.064, 3.125)\n- R80: Q3 1.6 (0.020, 0.032, 2.000), Q3 2.5 (0.031, 0.050, 3.125)\n- R100: Q3 1.6 (0.016, 0.026, 2.000), Q3 2.5 (0.025, 0.040, 3.125)\n- R125: Q3 1.6 (0.013, 0.021, 2.000), Q3 2.5 (0.020, 0.032, 3.125)\n- R160: Q3 1.6 (0.010, 0.016), Q3 2.5 (0.016, 0.025)\n- R200: Q3 1.6 (0.008, 0.013), Q3 2.5 (0.013, 0.020)\n- R250: Q3 1.6 (0.006, 0.010), Q3 2.5 (0.010, 0.016)\n- R315: Q3 1.6 (0.005, 0.008), Q3 2.5 (0.008, 0.013)\n- R400: Q3 1.6 (0.004, 0.006), Q3 2.5 (0.006, 0.010)\n- R500: Q3 1.6 (0.003, 0.005), Q3 2.5 (0.005, 0.008)\n- R630: Q3 1.6 (0.0025, 0.004), Q3 2.5 (0.004, 0.006)\n- R800: Q3 2.5 (0.003, 0.005)\n- R1000: Q3 2.5 (0.0025, 0.004)\n\nThat's 14×2 + 2 = 30? Wait: ratios 40–630 have both Q3 values: 40,50,63,80,100,125 (6 ratios ×2 = 12), 160–500 (6 ratios ×2 = 12), 630 (×2 = 2), 800 (×1), 1000 (×1). Total = 28. Good.\n\nDN20:\n- R40: Q3 2.5 (0.063, 0.100, 3.125), Q3 4.0 (0.100, 0.160, 5.00)\n- R50: Q3 2.5 (0.050, 0.080), Q3 4.0 (0.080, 0.128)\n- R63: Q3 2.5 (0.040, 0.064), Q3 4.0 (0.064, 0.102)\n- R80: Q3 2.5 (0.031, 0.050), Q3 4.0 (0.050, 0.080)\n- R100: Q3 2.5 (0.025, 0.040), Q3 4.0 (0.040, 0.064)\n- R125: Q3 2.5 (0.020, 0.032), Q3 4.0 (0.032, 0.051)\n- R160: Q3 2.5 (0.016, 0.025), Q3 4.0 (0.025, 0.040)\n- R200: Q3 2.5 (0.013, 0.020), Q3 4.0 (0.020, 0.032)\n- R250: Q3 2.5 (0.010, 0.016), Q3 4.0 (0.016, 0.026)\n- R315: Q3 2.5 (0.008, 0.013), Q3 4.0 (0.013, 0.020)\n- R400: Q3 2.5 (0.006, 0.010), Q3 4.0 (0.010, 0.016)\n- R500: Q3 2.5 (0.005, 0.008), Q3 4.0 (0.008, 0.013)\n- R630: Q3 2.5 (0.004, 0.006)\n- R800: Q3 4.0 (0.005, 0.008)\n- R1000: Q3 4.0 (0.004, 0.006)\n\nTotal 28.\n\nCombined 56 models.\n\nThat's a lot but doable. Let me define model_id format: `DN15-Q3=1.6-R40`? Or following the pattern in the instructions (e.g. \"G2.5\"), maybe \"DN15 Q3 1.6 R40\". I'll use `model_id: \"DN15_Q3-1.6_R40\"`. Hmm. Let me just use \"DN15 Q3=1.6 Q3/Q1=40\"? Too verbose. I'll go with `DN15-Q3 1.6-R40` style... Actually cleanest: `DN15_1.6_R40`. But then it loses clarity that 1.6 is Q3. I'll use `DN15-Q3=1.6-R=40`.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider. The instructions example: `{\"model_id\": \"Emax=25t\"}` and `{\"model_id\": \"G1.6\"}`. So attribute=value style is fine. I'll use `model_id: \"DN15 Q3=1.6 R=40\"`. Labels: label could be \"sonico NANO DN15, Q3 = 1.6 m³/h, Q3/Q1 = 40\". I'll include label maybe same as model_id or null. Keep it simple: model_id string only, maybe with label.\n\nFor model_level characteristics, I'd need q1, q2, q3, q4, q3_q1_ratio per model — 56 entries per attribute × 5 attributes = 280 entries. That's a lot of JSON but fine. Actually, maybe I should be more economical: the matrix_tables capture the full grid. The model_level is \"MOST IMPORTANT\" per instructions. I'll do it — 56 models per attribute is big but let me do it for q1, q2, q3, q4, and q3_q1_ratio. Also nominal_diameter and pressure_loss_class and connection_type as model_level (varying by DN).\n\nAlternatively, I could define model_id as just the ratio+Q3+DN combos and put q-values in matrix_tables only, with model_level for nominal_diameter, q3, q3_q1_ratio, pressure_loss_class, connection_type. Hmm.\n\nThe rules say: populate model_family.models AND characteristics.model_level for 2-D tables. I'll do a pragmatic middle ground:\n\n- matrix_tables: 6 tables preserved as-is (rows per ratio group).\n- model_family.models: 56 entries.\n- characteristics.model_level: \n  - nominal_diameter (56 values) — or this is derivable from model_id; but include it.\n  - q3 (56), q3_q1_ratio (56), q1 (56), q2 (56), q4 (56)\n  - pressure_loss_class (string values like \"Δp10\" per model — 28 unique pairs; varies by DN and Q3)\n  - connection_type (varies by DN)\n  - temperature_class? No, type-level T50.\n\nThat's huge but correct. Let me weigh output size: each value entry ~ 60 bytes → 56×6 ≈ 336 entries × ~70 bytes ≈ 24KB. Plus matrix tables. Acceptable? The instructions emphasize completeness. Let me do it but keep values compact.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: maybe keep model_level to the primary metrological attributes (q1, q2, q3, q4, q3_over_q1, nominal_diameter) and put pressure_loss_class and connection_type in config_level or model_level too. Pressure loss class depends on DN and Q3: DN15 Q3=1.6 → Δp10; DN15 Q3=2.5 → Δp16; DN20 Q3=2.5 → Δp16; DN20 Q3=4.0 → Δp25. Connection type depends on DN.\n\nI could use config_level for these:\n- attribute: pressure_loss_class, axis: nominal_diameter_and_q3, values: [{condition: \"DN15 Q3=1.6\", value: \"Δp10\"}, ...]\n- attribute: connection_type, axis: nominal_diameter, values: [{condition: \"DN15\", value: \"NPSM or G type ¾\\\", 7/8\\\", 1\\\"\"}, {condition: \"DN20\", value: \"NPSM or G type 7/8\\\", 1\\\", 1 1/4\\\"\"}]\n\nHmm, but per-model is more consistent. Let me include pressure_loss_class in model_level per model too. That's another 56. Total model_level entries: 8 attributes × 56 = 448 entries. That's getting really big. Let me trim: include nominal_diameter, q1, q2, q3, q4, q3_q1_ratio, pressure_loss_class, connection_type. Actually connection type per model is repetitive; config_level is more apt for DN-dependent attributes. But consistency with per-model matrix... The instruction #5 says config axes for attributes varying by configuration. Connection type and pressure loss vary by DN/Q3 configuration which is essentially the model itself. Since models already encode DN/Q3/R, pressure_loss_class belongs per model.\n\nDecision: model_level with attributes: nominal_diameter (mm, dimensionless — nominal diameter in mm: 15, 20), q1, q2, q3, q4 (m³/h), q3_q1_ratio (dimensionless), q2_q1_ratio (1.6, type-level), q4_q3_ratio (1.25, type-level), pressure_loss_class (string per model), connection_type via config_level axis nominal_diameter.\n\nActually let me simplify: put connection_type in config_level. Good.\n\nLet me now also handle type-level characteristics:\n- measuring_principle: \"ultrasonic\"\n- accuracy_class: 2\n- mpe_lower_flowrate_zone: ±5% → value 5, unit \"%\", footnote? It's \"±5 %\". Represent value: 5, unit \"%\", with maybe note sign ±. I'll just use 5.\n- mpe_upper_flowrate_zone: ±2% for temp ≤30°C, ±3% for >30°C → config_level with axis \"water_temperature\": [{condition: \"≤ 30 °C\", value: 2, unit \"%\"}, {condition: \"> 30 °C\", value: 3, unit: \"%\"}]\n- temperature_class: \"T50\"\n- water_pressure_class: \"MAP16\"\n- reverse_flow: \"designed to measure\"\n- environmental_class: \"M\"\n- electromagnetic_environment: \"E2\"\n- maximum_admissible_temperature: 50 °C\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6 MPa\n- orientation_limitation: \"any\"\n- indicating_range_testing_mode: 9999 m³; indicating_range_user_mode: 999999 m³ → could be config_level axis \"mode\": [{condition: \"testing mode\", value 9999}, {condition: \"user mode\", value 999999}]. Or two type-level attrs. I'll do config_level with axis \"indicating_mode\". Similarly resolution: 0.00001 (testing) / 0.001 (user) m³ config_level.\n- resolution_of_device_for_rapid_testing: 100 pulse/dm³\n- resolution_of_indicating_device_for_rapid_testing: 0.000001 m³\n- eut_category: \"B\"; eut_case: \"B\"\n- min_straight_length_inlet_pipe: 0 mm; outlet: 0 mm\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0D0\"\n- flow_conditioner_required: \"No\"\n- length: ≥105 mm → {min: 105, max: null} mm\n- power_supply_type: \"Battery\"\n- battery_voltage_max: 3.6 V (Umax), battery_voltage_min: 1.9 V (Umin)\n- minimum_battery_life_time: 16 years\n- software_version: \"0.6.28\"\n- crc_checksum: [\"0x854EBACF\", \"0x13372073\"] — two values. Value could be a list? Schema says value is number|range|string. Two CRCs... I could record crc_checksum_1 and crc_checksum_2, or a single string \"0x854EBACF, 0x13372073\". Hmm. I'll record two: crc_checksum_legal_software as string list? Strict shape says value must be number/minmax/string. I'll do two entries: crc_checksum_1: \"0x854EBACF\", crc_checksum_2: \"0x13372073\". Or one config_level axis \"software_configuration\". Simplest: two type-level attrs.\n\nWait, why two CRCs? Possibly two SW variants. The doc doesn't clarify. I'll emit crc_checksum entries as config_level axis \"software_variant\" with conditions... but conditions aren't given. I'll just do type_level: crc_checksum_1 and crc_checksum_2.\n\n- volume_resolution_display: 0.00001 m³ (from description)\n- impulse module: not part of certificate — skip or note in description.\n\nFootnote 1: \"The ratio Q3/Q1 shall be chosen according to paragraph 4.1.4 of OIML R 49-1:2013\". Marker \"1\". Referenced in Q3/Q1 cells: \"≤ 1000 1 for Q3=2.5m³/h, ≤ 630 1 for Q3=1.6m³/h\". So q3_q1 type-level has footnote marker \"1\". Actually those cells say the ratio ranges: for Q3=2.5, ratio ≤1000; for Q3=1.6, ratio ≤630. These are covered by the matrix. I could record as config_level:\n- attribute: maximum_ratio_q3_q1, axis: q3, values: [{condition: \"Q3=2.5 m³/h\", value: 1000, footnote_markers: [\"1\"]}, {condition: \"Q3=1.6 m³/h\", value: 630, footnote_markers: [\"1\"]}]. Hmm, DN20 Q3=4.0 max ratio is 1000 too. The page-4 table covers both DN columns with that one cell (rowspan 5 spanning both columns). Fine.\n\n**Revision history:** Revision \"-\" (initial issue), date 6 June 2024, changes \"Issuing certificate\". Schema: revision string. Use \"0\"? The table shows \"-\". Rule: revision string e.g. \"0\". The doc shows \"-\", meaning initial. I'll use \"-\" to be faithful. Hmm, example says e.g. \"0\" not 0. \"-\" is what document shows; keep \"-\".\n\n**Test reports:**\n- 0511-ER-V061-23, dated 29 May 2024, 32 pages, role: type_evaluation_report\n- 6015-PT-P5006-24, 206 pages, role: test_report\n- 6011-PT-SW008-24, 7 pages, role: test_report\n- Documentation file: 0511-UL-V061-23 → test_reports with role documentation_file? The schema has test_reports[] with role documentation_file. Yes, include: id \"0511-UL-V061-23\", role \"documentation_file\".\n\n**Recommendation:** id \"R49\", edition 2013, scheme \"A\", accuracy_classes [2].\n\n**Certificate:** number \"R49/2013-A-CZ1-24.02\", scheme \"A\", page_total 7, member_state \"Czech Republic\", date_issued \"2024-06-06\", project_number? Not given. oiml_issuer_id? Not explicitly given. Omit.\n\nDate format: ISO \"2024-06-06\".\n\nNow, model_id naming. Let me settle: `DN15-Q3=1.6-R=40`. Hmm, slashes in \"Q3/Q1\" would be awkward. Use \"R\" for ratio. Final: `\"DN15 Q3=1.6 R=40\"`? I'll use hyphens: `DN15-Q3=1.6-R=40`. Consistent and parseable.\n\nActually, considering readability, maybe `DN15_Q3_1.6_R40`. I'll go with `DN15-Q3=1.6-R40`.\n\nLet me generate the model list systematically.\n\nDN15 (Q3 = 1.6 for ratios 40,50,63,80,100,125,160,200,250,315,400,500,630; Q3 = 2.5 for ratios 40..800... wait no.\n\nDN15 models (28):\n1. DN15-Q3=1.6-R40: q1 0.040, q2 0.064, q4 2.000\n2. DN15-Q3=2.5-R40: q1 0.063, q2 0.100, q4 3.125\n3. DN15-Q3=1.6-R50: q1 0.032, q2 0.051, q4 2.000\n4. DN15-Q3=2.5-R50: q1 0.050, q2 0.080, q4 3.125\n5. DN15-Q3=1.6-R63: q1 0.025, q2 0.041, q4 2.000\n6. DN15-Q3=2.5-R63: q1 0.040, q2 0.064, q4 3.125\n7. DN15-Q3=1.6-R80: q1 0.020, q2 0.032, q4 2.000\n8. DN15-Q3=2.5-R80: q1 0.031, q2 0.050, q4 3.125\n9. DN15-Q3=1.6-R100: q1 0.016, q2 0.026, q4 2.000\n10. DN15-Q3=2.5-R100: q1 0.025, q2 0.040, q4 3.125\n11. DN15-Q3=1.6-R125: q1 0.013, q2 0.021, q4 2.000\n12. DN15-Q3=2.5-R125: q1 0.020, q2 0.032, q4 3.125\n13. DN15-Q3=1.6-R160: q1 0.010, q2 0.016, q4 2.000\n14. DN15-Q3=2.5-R160: q1 0.016, q2 0.025, q4 3.125\n15. DN15-Q3=1.6-R200: q1 0.008, q2 0.013, q4 2.000\n16. DN15-Q3=2.5-R200: q1 0.013, q2 0.020, q4 3.125\n17. DN15-Q3=1.6-R250: q1 0.006, q2 0.010, q4 2.000\n18. DN15-Q3=2.5-R250: q1 0.010, q2 0.016, q4 3.125\n19. DN15-Q3=1.6-R315: q1 0.005, q2 0.008, q4 2.000\n20. DN15-Q3=2.5-R315: q1 0.008, q2 0.013, q4 3.125\n21. DN15-Q3=1.6-R400: q1 0.004, q2 0.006, q4 2.000\n22. DN15-Q3=2.5-R400: q1 0.006, q2 0.010, q4 3.125\n23. DN15-Q3=1.6-R500: q1 0.003, q2 0.005, q4 2.000\n24. DN15-Q3=2.5-R500: q1 0.005, q2 0.008, q4 3.125\n25. DN15-Q3=1.6-R630: q1 0.0025, q2 0.004, q4 2.000\n26. DN15-Q3=2.5-R630: q1 0.004, q2 0.