0 (colspan 2), 800, 1000. Q3 row: 2.500, 4.00 | 4.00 | 4.00. So 630 has both 2.5 (Q1 0.004) and 4.0 (Q1 0.006)! Let me recheck:\n\n```\n<tr><td><em>Q</em><sub>1</sub> [m³/h]:</td><td>0.004</td><td>0.006</td><td>0.005</td><td>0.004</td></tr>\n...\n<tr><td><em>Q</em><sub>3</sub> [m³/h]:</td><td>2.500</td><td>4.00</td><td>4.00</td><td>4.00</td></tr>\n...\n<tr><td><em>Q</em><sub>3</sub>/<em>Q</em><sub>1</sub>:</td><td colspan=\"2\" align=\"center\">630</td><td align=\"center\">800</td><td align=\"center\">1000</td></tr>\n```\n\nSo DN20 R630: Q3 2.5 → Q1 0.004; Q3 4.0 → Q1 0.006. And R800: Q3 4.0 → Q1 0.005; R1000: Q3 4.0 → Q1 0.004. So DN20 has 28 too. \n\nDN20 models:\n53. DN20-Q3=2.5-R630: q1 0.004, q2 0.006, q4 3.125\n54. DN20-Q3=4.0-R630: q1 0.006, q2 0.010, q4 5.0\n55. DN20-Q3=4.0-R800: q1 0.005, q2 0.008, q4 5.0\n56. DN20-Q3=4.0-R1000: q1 0.004, q2 0.006, q4 5.0\n\nTotal 56 models.\n\nPressure loss class per model:\n- DN15 Q3=1.6 → Δp10\n- DN15 Q3=2.5 → Δp16\n- DN20 Q3=2.5 → Δp16\n- DN20 Q3=4.0 → Δp25\n\nConnection type per DN:\n- DN15: NPSM or G type ¾\", 7/8\", 1\"\n- DN20: NPSM or G type 7/8\", 1\", 1 1/4\"\n\nLength ≥105 mm for both DN.\n\nNow matrix_tables. I'll produce 6 tables preserving the original structure. Each row is an object; columns: attribute rows. But the tables are transposed (attributes as rows, values across). The schema example for matrix_tables has columns as attributes and rows as models. I could restructure: for each table, rows = ratio groups with q3/q1 ratio, and q1/q2/q3/q4 for each of the two Q3 variants. That's awkward. Better: restructure into model rows:\n\nFor table 1 (DN15, R40–R125), 12 model rows:\ncolumns: [\"nominal_diameter\", \"q3_m3_h\", \"q3_q1_ratio\", \"q1_m3_h\", \"q2_m3_h\", \"q4_m3_h\"]\n\nThat transforms the transposed table into model-per-row. That matches schema intent (\"rows[] (object per row)\"). I'll do that for all 6 tables, with description noting original transposed layout. Alternatively keep original layout