ow: \"designed to measure\"\n- environmental_class: \"M\"\n- electromagnetic_environment: \"E2\"\n- maximum_admissible_temperature: 50 °C\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6 MPa\n- orientation_limitation: \"any\"\n- indicating_range: config_level axis \"mode\": testing 9999 m³, user 999999 m³\n- indicating_device_resolution: config_level axis \"mode\": testing 0.00001 m³, user 0.001 m³\n- rapid_testing_device_resolution: 100 pulse/dm³\n- rapid_testing_indicating_device_resolution: 0.000001 m³\n- eut_test_category: \"B\"\n- eut_test_case: \"B\"\n- connection_type: config_level axis nominal_diameter\n- min_straight_length_inlet_pipe: 0 mm\n- min_straight_length_outlet_pipe: 0 mm\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0D0\"\n- flow_conditioner_required: \"No\"\n- length: {min:105, max:null} mm\n- power_supply_type: \"Battery\"\n- battery_voltage_max (u_max): 3.6 V\n- battery_voltage_min (u_min): 1.9 V\n- minimum_battery_life: 16 years (unit \"years\" → unit_symbol \"a\"? Use \"years\" raw)\n- software_version: \"0.6.28\"\n- crc_checksum: config-level? I'll do two type-level entries crc_checksum_1/2. Or one with value string \"0x854EBACF / 0x13372073\". Rules say value number|minmax|string; a combined string is acceptable but loses structure. I'll do config_level axis \"software_variant\"? Conditions unknown. Simpler: crc_checksum_1, crc_checksum_2. Hmm, actually maybe there are two SW builds. I'll emit two separate characteristics.\n- q2_q1_ratio: 1.6\n- q4_q3_ratio: 1.25\n- display_volume_resolution: 0.00001 m³ (from description) — same as testing-mode resolution; skip to avoid duplication? It's stated in description; the resolution table covers it. Skip.\n\nFootnotes: [{marker: \"1\", text: \"The ratio Q3/Q1 shall be chosen according to paragraph 4.1.4 of OIML R 49-1:2013\"}]\n\nRevision history: [{revision: \"-\", date: \"2024-06-06\", changes: \"Issuing certificate\"}]\n\nTest reports:\n- {id: \"0511-ER-V061-23\", date: \"2024-05-29\", pages: 32, role: \"type_evaluation_report\"}\n- {id: \"6015-PT-P5006-24\", pages: 206, role: \"test_report\"}\n- {id: \"6011-PT-SW008-24\", pages: 7, role: \"test_report\"}\n- {id: \"0511-UL-V061-23\", role: \"documentation_file\"}\n\nRecommendation: {id: \"R49\", edition: 2013, scheme: \"A\", accuracy_classes: [2]}\n\nCertificate: number \"R49/2013-A-CZ1-24.02\", scheme \"A\", page_total 7, member_state \"Czech Republic\", date_issued \"2024-06-06\".\n\nIssuing authority: name \"Czech Metrology Institute\", address_lines [\"Okružní 31\", \"638 00 Brno\", \"Czech Republic\"], person_responsible \"Jan Kalandra\". Also signed by RNDr. Pavel Klenovský, Head of Certification Body — schema only has person_responsible/person_title. The explicit \"Person responsible:\" is Jan Kalandra. I could add person_title null. Or add signed_by? Not in schema; omit. Hmm — maybe include \"person_responsible\": \"Jan Kalandra\" only.\n\ncertified_type:\n- category: \"Water meter\"\n- type_designations: [\"sonico NANO\"]\n- module_designation: \"-\" → The doc shows \"-\" meaning none. Set to null or \"Not applicable\". Schema options: \"Analog load cell\" | \"Not applicable\" | null. Use null? \"-\" means not applicable. I'll use \"Not applicable\". Actually the field says include if applicable; \"-\" = not applicable → null is fine too. I'll put null... Hmm. Let me put \"Not applicable\" since the cert explicitly marks it as not applicable. Either fine. I'll go null. Wait: rule 8 says don't fabricate; module_designation \"-\" clearly indicates none. I'll use null. Hmm, but expressing the fact might be useful. The schema enum suggests \"Not applicable\" is a valid value. Use \"Not applicable\".\n\n- description: summarizing the description text.\n\ncomponents: The water meter consists of body, ultrasonic transducers, electronic indicating device — but these are integral parts, not separately certified components. The cert doesn't name separate sub-assembly type designations. Skip components.\n\nmodel_family: family_name: \"sonico NANO\", models: [56 entries].\n\nmodel_level attributes: nominal_diameter (mm? nominal diameter unit mm — DN15 means 15mm; unit \"mm\"), q1, q2, q3, q4 (m³/h), q3_q1_ratio (dimensionless), pressure_loss_class (string).\n\nThat's 7 × 56. Let me also decide whether to add connection_type to model_level per model — it's DN-dependent; config_level covers it. Fine.\n\nNow write the JSON. Values: keep numbers as given, e.g. 0.04 (JSON drops trailing zero), 1.6, 3.125, 5, 4.\n\nLet me build the model_level arrays carefully.\n\nI'll write them in the order listed above.\n\nFor brevity in my head, define order list:\n\nDN15 (28):\n1 DN15-Q3=1.6-R40\n2 DN15-Q3=2.5-R40\n3 DN15-Q3=1.6-R50\n4 DN15-Q3=2.5-R50\n5 DN15-Q3=1.6-R63\n6 DN15-Q3=2.5-R63\n7 DN15-Q3=1.6-R80\n8 DN15-Q3=2.5-R80\n9 DN15-Q3=1.6-R100\n10 DN15-Q3=2.5-R100\n11 DN15-Q3=1.6-R125\n12 DN15-Q3=2.5-R125\n13 DN15-Q3=1.6-R160\n14 DN15-Q3=2.5-R160\n15 DN15-Q3=1.6-R200\n16 DN15-Q3=2.5-R200\n17 DN15-Q3=1.6-R250\n18 DN15-Q3=2.5-R250\n19 DN15-Q3=1.6-R315\n20 DN15-Q3=2.5-R315\n21 DN15-Q3=1.6-R400\n22 DN15-Q3=2.5-R400\n23 DN15-Q3=1.6-R500\n24 DN15-Q3=2.5-R500\n25 DN15-Q3=1.6-R630\n26 DN15-Q3=2.5-R630\n27 DN15-Q3=2.5-R800\n28 DN15-Q3=2.5-R1000\n\nDN20 (28):\n29 DN20-Q3=2.5-R40\n30 DN20-Q3=4.0-R40\n31 DN20-Q3=2.5-R50\n32 DN20-Q3=4.0-R50\n33 DN20-Q3=2.5-R63\n34 DN20-Q3=4.0-R63\n35 DN20-Q3=2.5-R80\n36 DN20-Q3=4.0-R80\n37 DN20-Q3=2.5-R100\n38 DN20-Q3=4.0-R100\n39 DN20-Q3=2.5-R125\n40 DN20-Q3=4.0-R125\n41 DN20-Q3=2.5-R160\n42 DN20-Q3=4.0-R160\n43 DN20-Q3=2.5-R200\n44 DN20-Q3=4.0-R200\n45 DN20-Q3=2.5-R250\n46 DN20-Q3=4.0-R250\n47 DN20-Q3=2.5-R315\n48 DN20-Q3=4.0-R315\n49 DN20-Q3=2.5-R400\n50 DN20-Q3=4.0-R400\n51 DN20-Q3=2.5-R500\n52 DN20-Q3=4.0-R500\n53 DN20-Q3=2.5-R630\n54 DN20-Q3=4.0-R630\n55 DN20-Q3=4.0-R800\n56 DN20-Q3=4.0-R1000\n\nOK. Now build everything. This will be long but let's go.\n\nmatrix_tables — 6 tables. Columns: [\"model_id\",\"nominal_diameter\",\"q3_q1_ratio\",\"q3\",\"q1\",\"q2\",\"q4\"]. column_units: [null, \"mm\", null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\"].\n\nWait, example includes column_units. Yes schema example shows column_units. The yaml schema lists name, columns[], rows[], description, footnotes[] — example JSON adds column_units. I'll include column_units.\n\nTable 1 rows (DN15 R40–125):\n{\"model_id\":\"DN15-Q3=1.6-R40\",\"nominal_diameter\":15,\"q3_q1_ratio\":40,\"q3\":1.6,\"q1\":0.040,\"q2\":0.064,\"q4\":2.0}\n{\"model_id\":\"DN15-Q3=2.5-R40\",\"nominal_diameter\":15,\"q3_q1_ratio\":40,\"q3\":2.5,\"q1\":0.063,\"q2\":0.100,\"q4\":3.125}\n... etc for R50,R63,R80,R100,R125.\n\nTable 2 (DN15 R160–500): 12 rows.\nTable 3 (DN15 R630–1000): 4 rows (630×2, 800, 1000).\nTable 4 (DN20 R40–125): 12 rows.\nTable 5 (DN20 R160–500): 12 rows.\nTable 6 (DN20 R630–1000): 4 rows.\n\nAlright, also record unit Symbol \"m³/h\" using the ³ character.\n\nAlso the recommendation accuracy_classes: [\"2\"]? Schema: accuracy_classes[] — numbers or strings? Example not given. Class \"2\" — use [\"2\"] as strings? For R60 example accuracy classes are like 0.2 etc. I'll use [2]? Water meter class 2 is typically written \"2\". I'll use [\"2\"]... Hmm. recommendation.accuracy_classes — I'll use [\"2\"]. Actually numeric 2 could be confused. String \"2\" is fine.\n\ncharacteristics.type_level entries with structured values. Let me define:\n\n- model_name: {value: \"sonico NANO\"}\n- accuracy_class: {value: 2}\n- mpe_lower_flowrate_zone: {value: 5, unit_symbol: \"%\"} — hmm, ±5%. Should I preserve sign? I'll leave value 5.\n- temperature_class: {value: \"T50\"}\n- water_pressure_class: {value: \"MAP16\"}\n- reverse_flow_capability: {value: \"designed to measure\"}\n- environmental_class: {value: \"M\"}\n- electromagnetic_environment: {value: \"E2\"}\n- maximum_admissible_temperature: {value: 50, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: {value: 1.6, unit_symbol: \"MPa\"}\n- orientation_limitation: {value: \"any\"}\n- rapid_testing_device_resolution: {value: 100, unit_symbol: \"pulse/dm³\"}\n- rapid_testing_indicating_device_resolution: {value: 0.000001, unit_symbol: \"m³\"}\n- eut_test_category: {value: \"B\"}\n- eut_test_case: {value: \"B\"}\n- min_straight_length_inlet_pipe: {value: 0, unit_symbol: \"mm\"}\n- min_straight_length_outlet_pipe: {value: 0, unit_symbol: \"mm\"}\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: {value: \"U0D0\"}\n- flow_conditioner_required: {value: \"No\"}\n- length: {value: {min:105, max:null}, unit_symbol: \"mm\"}\n- power_supply_type: {value: \"Battery\"}\n- battery_voltage_max: {value: 3.6, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n- battery_voltage_min: {value: 1.9, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n- minimum_battery_life: {value: 16, unit_symbol: \"years\"}\n- software_version: {value: \"0.6.28\"}\n- crc_checksum_1: {value: \"0x854EBACF\"}\n- crc_checksum_2: {value: \"0x13372073\"}\n- q2_q1_ratio: {value: 1.6}\n- q4_q3_ratio: {value: 1.25}\n\nconfig_level:\n- mpe_upper_flowrate_zone, axis \"water_temperature\": [{condition: \"≤ 30 °C\", value: 2, unit_symbol:\"%\"}, {condition: \"> 30 °C\", value: 3, unit_symbol:\"%\"}]\n- indicating_range, axis \"mode\": [{condition: \"testing mode\", value: 9999, unit_symbol: \"m³\"}, {condition: \"user mode\", value: 999999, unit_symbol: \"m³\"}]\n- indicating_device_resolution, axis \"mode\": [{condition \"testing mode\", value 0.00001, unit \"m³\"}, {condition \"user mode\", value 0.001, unit \"m³\"}]\n- connection_type, axis \"nominal_diameter\": [{condition \"DN15\", value \"NPSM or G type ¾\\\", 7/8\\\", 1\\\"\"}, {condition \"DN20\", value \"NPSM or G type 7/8\\\", 1\\\", 1 1/4\\\"\"}]\n- maximum_ratio_q3_over_q1, axis \"q3\": [{condition \"Q3 = 1.6 m³/h\", value 630, footnote_markers [\"1\"]}, {condition \"Q3 = 2.5 m³/h\", value 1000, footnote_markers [\"1\"]}]\n\nHmm, the \"1 1/4\\\"\" contains quotes — in JSON escape as \\\".\n\nFor the condition keys in config_level: the schema says values: {condition, value}. I'll include unit_symbol too as per rule 5 (\"Values follow the same structured form: {condition, value, unit_symbol}\").\n\nmodel_level: 7 attributes. Write them out.\n\nnominal_diameter: unit \"mm\". Values 15 ×28, 20 ×28.\n\nq3: unit \"m³/h\". Values per order:\nDN15: 1.6,2.5 alternating for R40..R630 (13 ratios → 26), then 2.5, 2.5.\nDN20: 2.5,4.0 alternating for R40..R630 (13 → 26), then 4.0, 4.0.\n\nq3_q1_ratio: DN15: 40,40,50,50,63,63,80,80,100,100,125,125,160,160,200,200,250,250,315,315,400,400,500,500,630,630,800,1000. DN20: same pattern with last four: 630,630,800,1000.\n\nq1 values in order (as above lists).\nq2 values.\nq4 values.\n\npressure_loss_class: DN15: Δp10 for 1.6 entries (13), Δp16 for 2.5 entries (15); DN20: Δp16 for 2.5 entries (13), Δp25 for 4.0 entries (15).\n\nLet me now also decide unit_id: leave null everywhere (rule: only if certain). I'll omit unit_id or set null? Rule 2 shape includes unit_id; rule 3 says leave null unless certain. I'll include \"unit_id\": null where unit present, and omit? The shape shows unit_id in the object. I'll include unit_id: null consistently. Hmm, that adds bulk. The strict shape says every value must be object with that shape. I'll include unit_id: null. Actually to reduce size maybe omit unit_id — \"Leave unit_id: null unless you are certain\" implies the field exists. I'll include it as null. That's a lot of nulls... For compactness I might omit unit_id where null. The instruction: \"Leave unit_id: null unless you are certain of the unitsdb ID.\" I'll include unit_id only when I set it; otherwise omit. Risk: schema strictness. The rule says every characteristic value MUST be an object with this shape — including unit_id. Ugh. To be safe, include \"unit_id\": null everywhere. That's ~448 extra nulls ≈ 7KB. Acceptable.\n\nHmm, actually, let me include it. Being compliant > brevity.\n\nActually wait — for model_level values, the example in the rules shows `{\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5}` without unit_symbol/unit_id in values — units are on the attribute level. So model_level values don't need unit_symbol per value; the attribute entry has unit_symbol. Good — example: {\"attribute\": \"qmax\", \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\", \"values\": [{\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5}...]}. So values are compact {model, value} or {model, value, footnote_markers}. \n\nBut rule 2 says every characteristic value must be object with value/unit_symbol/unit_id... The examples override for model_level. I'll follow the examples: model_level attribute has unit_symbol at attribute level; values are {model, value}. config_level values include unit_symbol per rule 5. type_level values have full shape {value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers?}.\n\nFor strings like \"T50\", shape: {\"value\": \"T50\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null}.\n\nOK. Let me also do matrix_tables rows — include model_id? The example rows don't include model_id ({\"size\": \"G2.5\", \"qmax\":...}) but including it is fine and helps. I'll include.\n\nNow description text: \"The water meters type sonico NANO are ultrasonic water meters with an electronic indicating device designed to measure, memorise and display the volume at metering conditions of water passing through the measurement transducer. They consist of a cast brass body with connecting screw threads, one pair of ultrasonic transducers and the electronic indicating device (LCD display showing volume and flow). Display volume resolution 0.00001 m³. No buttons; legally non-relevant communication possible via NFC sensor. Software separation; CRC and SW version shown in auto-rounding menu. Indicates delivered volume for reverse and forward flow separately every two minutes; permanently shown volume is the difference. Can be equipped by impulse module which is not part of this certificate. Powered by 3.6 V mains battery. Shall be installed to operate in any positions.\"\n\nCondense reasonably.\n\nCertificate project_number: not present → omit. oiml_issuer_id: not present → omit.\n\nAlso \"Verze 20-001\" is a form version — ignore.\n\nNow, assemble final JSON. Let me be careful with escaping the inch marks: `¾”` uses curly quote in doc (\"¾”, 7/8”, 1”\"). I'll keep the curly quotes: 'NPSM or G type ¾\", 7/8\", 1\"' — use straight quotes escaped. Original: `NPSM or G type ¾”, 7/8”, 1”`. I'll normalize to straight double quotes escaped in JSON: \"NPSM or G type ¾\\\", 7/8\\\", 1\\\"\". Fine.\n\nLet me now write everything out.\n\nCertificate number: \"R49/2013-A-CZ1-24.02\".\n\ndate_issued: \"2024-06-06\".\n\nLet me now write model_family models with optional label. I'll add label like \"sonico NANO DN15, Q3 = 1.6 m³/h, Q3/Q1 = 40\"? That's heavy ×56. Skip labels or use null. I'll just give model_id and minimal attributes maybe not needed. Just model_id.\n\nNow generate model_level arrays. Let me write them.\n\nq1 (m³/h) in order:\n1 DN15-Q3=1.6-R40: 0.04\n2 DN15-Q3=2.5-R40: 0.063\n3 DN15-Q3=1.6-R50: 0.032\n4 DN15-Q3=2.5-R50: 0.05\n5 DN15-Q3=1.6-R63: 0.025\n6 DN15-Q3=2.5-R63: 0.04\n7 DN15-Q3=1.6-R80: 0.02\n8 DN15-Q3=2.5-R80: 0.031\n9 DN15-Q3=1.6-R100: 0.016\n10 DN15-Q3=2.5-R100: 0.025\n11 DN15-Q3=1.6-R125: 0.013\n12 DN15-Q3=2.5-R125: 0.02\n13 DN15-Q3=1.6-R160: 0.01\n14 DN15-Q3=2.5-R160: 0.016\n15 DN15-Q3=1.6-R200: 0.008\n16 DN15-Q3=2.5-R200: 0.013\n17 DN15-Q3=1.6-R250: 0.006\n18 DN15-Q3=2.5-R250: 0.01\n19 DN15-Q3=1.6-R315: 0.005\n20 DN15-Q3=2.5-R315: 0.008\n21 DN15-Q3=1.6-R400: 0.004\n22 DN15-Q3=2.5-R400: 0.006\n23 DN15-Q3=1.6-R500: 0.003\n24 DN15-Q3=2.5-R500: 0.005\n25 DN15-Q3=1.6-R630: 0.0025\n26 DN15-Q3=2.5-R630: 0.004\n27 DN15-Q3=2.5-R800: 0.003\n28 DN15-Q3=2.5-R1000: 0.0025\n29 DN20-Q3=2.5-R40: 0.063\n30 DN20-Q3=4.0-R40: 0.1\n31 DN20-Q3=2.5-R50: 0.05\n32 DN20-Q3=4.0-R50: 0.08\n33 DN20-Q3=2.5-R63: 0.04\n34 DN20-Q3=4.0-R63: 0.064\n35 DN20-Q3=2.5-R80: 0.031\n36 DN20-Q3=4.0-R80: 0.05\n37 DN20-Q3=2.5-R100: 0.025\n38 DN20-Q3=4.0-R100: 0.04\n39 DN20-Q3=2.5-R125: 0.02\n40 DN20-Q3=4.0-R125: 0.032\n41 DN20-Q3=2.5-R160: 0.016\n42 DN20-Q3=4.0-R160: 0.025\n43 DN20-Q3=2.5-R200: 0.013\n44 DN20-Q3=4.0-R200: 0.02\n45 DN20-Q3=2.5-R250: 0.01\n46 DN20-Q3=4.0-R250: 0.016\n47 DN20-Q3=2.5-R315: 0.008\n48 DN20-Q3=4.0-R315: 0.013\n49 DN20-Q3=2.5-R400: 0.006\n50 DN20-Q3=4.0-R400: 0.01\n51 DN20-Q3=2.5-R500: 0.005\n52 DN20-Q3=4.0-R500: 0.008\n53 DN20-Q3=2.5-R630: 0.004\n54 DN20-Q3=4.0-R630: 0.006\n55 DN20-Q3=4.0-R800: 0.005\n56 DN20-Q3=4.0-R1000: 0.004\n\nq2 = q1 × 1.6, check:\nDN15 R40 1.6: 0.040×1.6=0.064 ✓. R40 2.5: 0.063×1.6=0.1008→0.100 ✓ (rounded).\nq2 list:\n1: 0.064\n2: 0.1\n3: 0.051\n4: 0.08\n5: 0.041\n6: 0.064\n7: 0.032\n8: 0.05\n9: 0.026\n10: 0.04\n11: 0.021\n12: 0.032\n13: 0.016\n14: 0.025\n15: 0.013\n16: 0.02\n17: 0.01\n18: 0.016\n19: 0.008\n20: 0.013\n21: 0.006\n22: 0.01\n23: 0.005\n24: 0.008\n25: 0.004\n26: 0.006\n27: 0.005\n28: 0.004\n29: 0.1\n30: 0.16\n31: 0.08\n32: 0.128\n33: 0.064\n34: 0.102\n35: 0.05\n36: 0.08\n37: 0.04\n38: 0.064\n39: 0.032\n40: 0.051\n41: 0.025\n42: 0.04\n43: 0.02\n44: 0.032\n45: 0.016\n46: 0.026\n47: 0.013\n48: 0.02\n49: 0.01\n50: 0.016\n51: 0.008\n52: 0.013\n53: 0.006\n54: 0.01\n55: 0.008\n56: 0.006\n\nq3 list:\nmodels 1–26 (DN15 R40..R630): 1.6, 2.5 alternating ×13 → [1.6,2.5]×13\n27: 2.5, 28: 2.5\n29–54 (DN20 R40..R630): [2.5,4]×13\n55: 4, 56: 4\n\nq4 list: DN15 1.6 → 2; DN15 2.5 → 3.125; DN20 2.5 → 3.125; DN20 4 → 5.\n1: 2, 2: 3.125, alternating for 1–26; 27: 3.125, 28: 3.125; 29–54: 3.125, 5 alternating; 55: 5, 56: 5.\n\nratio list:\n1–28: 40,40,50,50,63,63,80,80,100,100,125,125,160,160,200,200,250,250,315,315,400,400,500,500,630,630,800,1000\n29–56: 40,40,50,50,63,63,80,80,100,100,125,125,160,160,200,200,250,250,315,315,400,400,500,500,630,630,800,1000\n\npressure_loss_class list:\n1–26: alternating Δp10 (odd positions 1,3,...25) and Δp16 (even), 27: Δp16, 28: Δp16; 29–54: alternating Δp16, Δp25; 55: Δp25, 56: Δp25.\n\nnominal_diameter: 1–28: 15; 29–56: 20.\n\nNow matrix tables:\n\nT1 (DN15, R40–R125), 12 rows:\nR40: (1.6: 0.040/0.064/1.6/2.0), (2.5: 0.063/0.100/2.5/3.125)\nR50: (0.032/0.051), (0.050/0.080)\nR63: (0.025/0.041), (0.